  "response": {
    "data": {
      "A": "Research a cache-friendly serial O(N²) replacement. Store input in one flat row-major array. Maintain N 64-bit column sums covering S consecutive rows; horizontally slide across these sums to evaluate every window, then update the columns using the exiting and entering matrix rows. This eliminates the baseline’s S-element work per horizontal move. Compare separate, compiler-vectorizable column updates against a fused update/scan variant. Initialize the maximum from an actual complete window, not zero. Produce an independently correct serial candidate and measure its end-to-end improvement before adding parallelism.",
      "B": "Research four-core CPU scaling without shared rolling-state dependencies. Partition the N-S+1 window-top rows into contiguous bands. Each worker initializes private column sums at its band’s starting row, processes that band using O(N²)-style rolling updates, and returns a local maximum for final reduction. Share only the read-only flat matrix; avoid per-window synchronization and false sharing. Compare one, two and four workers using only threading supported by the unchanged build. Account for duplicated O(SN) initialization per worker, memory bandwidth and launch overhead. Retain a general work-based serial fallback when parallelism cannot amortize these costs.",
      "C": "Research fast input and a standalone streaming O(N²) alternative. Use one buffered fread-based integer reader for the entire input; support signs, ordinary whitespace and EOF immediately after the last token. Do not mix unsynchronized C++ input with stdio. For each incoming row, compute its S-wide horizontal sums. Maintain their vertical totals using a circular buffer of S rows of horizontal sums, and update the maximum once S rows are available. Use 64-bit horizontal sums, vertical totals and differences. The ring requires 8*S*(N-S+1) bytes, at most approximately 128 MB under the stated bounds. Compare streaming against the same reader with flat-matrix storage, separating parser gains from layout gains where the existing budget permits.",
      "G": "Integrate only independently validated candidates. First compare A, B and C standalone; then evaluate minimal compatible hybrids, especially C’s reader with A’s serial kernel or B’s parallel kernel. Treat C’s streaming representation as an alternative, not something automatically compatible with B’s random-access row bands. Recheck correctness after every integration, including reasoning about S=1, S=N-1, negative-only matrices and overflowing 32-bit intermediate sums. Use the unchanged correctness and timing machinery within existing budgets; measure complete executables, including parsing, allocation and thread startup. Keep the best measured standalone candidate when combinations regress. Choose only repeatable end-to-end improvements, never summed component speedups or test-specific dispatch rules.",
      "analysis": "The supplied source implements official Problem B, maximum-sum S×S submatrix; the other statements are unrelated. Its serpentine traversal repeatedly accesses S matrix rows for each horizontal move, creating O(S(N-S+1)²) work and poor locality. The strongest initial opportunity is an exact O(N²) sliding-sum algorithm. Baseline measurements show correctness on four cases and a 0.126500-second mean, with samples spanning approximately 0.125629–0.127444 seconds; they provide no input dimensions or bottleneck breakdown. Consequently, parsing and parallel overhead deserve measurement, not assumptions. Target only the existing four-core ARM64 CPU. Across all roles, preserve every valid signed input and the single decimal output line. Use 64-bit accumulated sums and widen operands before subtraction: a 32-bit final maximum does not bound all intermediate sums. Keep tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target and permissions unchanged."
    },
